#{[17592186373196 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186373200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373196 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5193249-041d-5258-8bb5-af201644a8df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373197 #edb/ref 1080 "{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "daef00ec-f0af-55f1-a147-630d2fa39a6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373198 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373198 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373198 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bdba039a-b7e3-51de-b62c-a0a62757bb9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373199 #edb/ref 1080 "{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373199 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373199 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8bb2de73-e9a4-5123-8f86-f3a4b9582828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373200 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373200 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373200 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "410db49e-beeb-5275-9c59-fb5a569b8fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373201 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373201 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373201 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9006d78c-7e75-5c49-8257-5f1459617ff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e0/e0331b5d4c9fa3c823d6596c1ab147b8ee459feb4a4feaed3c614e2639ae77e5.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-xy$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\},$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f539d9c8-b40f-5e02-8e74-4380e9158356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373325 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-y,\\,-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-y &=0 \\\\ -x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186373326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373326 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186373331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 