=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+y)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+y)=x+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x-y)=-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x+1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(-2)(1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385242 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "afde4ae9-9caa-51ba-9a0b-0